---
import floors from '../../data/probe/floor.json'
import probe from '../../data/probe/results2.json'
import { median } from '../lib/metrics.js'

// The kill attempt, shown. Every figure is floor.json's or results2.json's, and the copy
// test recomputes each one from dataset.json as well, so a number that only one file
// supports cannot reach the page.
const PHILIP = 'Prince Philip, Duke of Edinburgh'
const row = floors.find((r) => r.article === PHILIP)
if (!row) throw new Error(`no floor.json row for ${PHILIP}`)

// results2.json stores the floor measured against the near window as res365, over the same
// days and the same peak anchor as the clean-window floor. It is the reading this beat is
// arguing down.
const probeOf = (name) => probe.find((r) => r.article === name)
const near = (name) => probeOf(name).res365
const nearBase = probeOf(PHILIP).baseline

// The rounding rule beat 1 states its percentages under, kept the same here so the page
// carries one rule. floor.json holds three decimals, so a row can land on an exact half.
const roundHalfEven = (x) => {
  const down = Math.trunc(x)
  const frac = x - down
  if (frac !== 0.5) return Math.round(x)
  return down % 2 === 0 ? down : down + 1
}
const pctOf = (floorValue) => roundHalfEven(Math.abs(floorValue) * 100)

const MONTHS = ['January', 'February', 'March', 'April', 'May', 'June',
                'July', 'August', 'September', 'October', 'November', 'December']
const [year, month, day] = row.event.split('-')
const died = `${Number(day)} ${MONTHS[Number(month) - 1]} ${year}`

const num = (n) => n.toLocaleString('en-US')

const deaths = floors.filter((r) => r.class === 'death')
const flips = deaths.filter((r) => Math.sign(r.floor) !== Math.sign(near(r.article))).length
const medRunup = median(deaths.map((r) => r.runup)).toFixed(2)

const belowNear = floors.filter((r) => near(r.article) < 0).length
const belowClean = floors.filter((r) => r.floor < 0).length
const share = (n) => Math.round((100 * n) / floors.length)
const up = floors.filter((r) => r.floor > near(r.article)).length
const down = floors.filter((r) => r.floor < near(r.article)).length

// The two baselines are measured medians and are stated. The level between them is drawn
// and not stated: floor.py wrote yr_later as the near baseline times one plus res365, and
// res365 is stored to two places, so the field is a reconstruction rather than a reading.
// dataset.json, which never went through that round trip, puts this subject at a different
// daily count.
const LEVELS = [
  { label: 'the window just before he died', value: nearBase, tone: 'near', show: true },
  { label: 'the same window a year earlier', value: row.clean_base, tone: 'clean', show: true },
  { label: 'a year after', value: row.yr_later, tone: 'after', show: false },
]
const TOP = Math.max(...LEVELS.map((l) => l.value))
---

<section class="beat">
  <p class="body">
    A floor needs a baseline. The obvious one is the window just before the event, and
    that is the one this started with.
  </p>

  <p class="body">
    The days just before an event are not ordinary days for a page.
  </p>

  <p class="body">
    {row.article} is the clearest case in the set. He died on {died}, after a month in hospital that February and March.
  </p>

  <figure class="fig">
    <div class="levels" role="img" aria-label="Daily views of the article across three windows: just before the event, the same window a year earlier, and a year after">
      {LEVELS.map((l) => (
        <div class="level" data-tone={l.tone}>
          <span class="lab">{l.label}</span>
          <span class="track"><span class="fill" style={`width:${(100 * l.value) / TOP}%`}></span></span>
          <span class="val">{l.show ? num(l.value) : ''}</span>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
    <figcaption>
      Daily views of his article. The window just before he died, the same window a year
      earlier, and where the article settled a year after.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <div class="pair">
    <div class="read">
      <p class="stat">{Math.round(Math.abs(near(PHILIP)) * 100)}%</p>
      <p class="cap">below the window just before he died</p>
    </div>
    <div class="read">
      <p class="stat">{pctOf(row.floor)}%</p>
      <p class="cap">below the window a year earlier</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="body">
    His near window ran at {row.runup} times his clean one. Across the {deaths.length} deaths that carry a floor the median is {medRunup} times, so this sits in particular subjects and not across the set.
  </p>

  <p class="body">
    {flips} of those {deaths.length} deaths change sign once the baseline is cleaned. The other {deaths.length - flips} hold.
  </p>

  <p class="body">
    Across all {floors.length} subjects the near window put {belowNear} of them below baseline, which is {share(belowNear)}%. The clean window puts {belowClean} below, which is {share(belowClean)}%.
  </p>

  <p class="body">
    The clean window does not move every subject the same way. {up} read higher against it and {down} read lower.
  </p>

  <p class="body">
    Every figure on this page uses the clean window.
  </p>
</section>

<style>
  .beat {
    padding-bottom: 6rem;
  }

  .body {
    max-width: var(--measure);
    margin: 2rem 0 0;
    font-size: 1.06rem;
  }

  .fig {
    margin: 2.5rem 0 0;
  }

  figcaption {
    max-width: var(--measure);
    margin: 1.2rem 0 0;
    font-size: 0.92rem;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }

  .levels {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.9rem;
  }

  .level {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 15rem) 1fr auto;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.9rem;
  }

  .lab {
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }

  .track {
    display: block;
    height: 18px;
  }

  .fill {
    display: block;
    height: 100%;
    background: var(--ink);
  }

  .level[data-tone='near'] .fill {
    background: var(--ink-soft);
  }

  .level[data-tone='after'] .fill {
    background: var(--below);
  }

  .val {
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .pair {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 2rem;
    max-width: 42rem;
    margin: 3rem 0 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
    padding-top: 1.5rem;
  }

  .stat {
    font-size: clamp(2.25rem, 7vw, 3.5rem);
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    line-height: 1;
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--below);
  }

  .cap {
    margin: 0.6rem 0 0;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }

  @media (max-width: 34rem) {
    .level {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr auto;
      gap: 0.35rem 0.9rem;
    }

    .lab {
      grid-column: 1 / -1;
    }

    .pair {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: 1.75rem;
    }
  }
</style>
